// **Does the collection trigger still keep a growing live set linear?**
//
// The trigger is `max(reserved / ZIMAN_GC_TRIGGER_DIVISOR, floor)` (src/interpreter.c). The two
// terms divide the work between them, and measuring which one *wins* is the only way to see it:
//
//     bench              peak arena   proportional won   floor won
//     map_insert                  0                  0   6,006,678
//     map_live                512 KB                 0   4,150,006
//     strings_live            512 KB                 0   4,300,006
//     structs_live           18.9 MB         4,099,357     100,649
//     decimal_churn          28.3 MB         1,699,357     100,649
//
// So the floor decides for every ordinary program -- it cannot be otherwise, since the proportional
// term only wins above twice the floor -- and the proportional term decides for a large live
// arena. **The other four benches here are all fixed-size**, which is the gap this one fills: none
// of them would notice the proportional term being removed, and a floor-only policy looks fine
// until the live set grows.
//
// Measured 2026-11-04 at a 4 MB floor, the same work under both policies:
//
//     live rows   divisor 2   floor only
//       200,000      118 ms       123 ms
//       400,000      267 ms       262 ms
//       800,000      489 ms       611 ms
//
// Linear against superlinear -- 1x/2.26x/4.14x for the first, 1x/2.13x/4.97x for the second, and
// the gap widens with every doubling. That is the quadratic the proportional term was added to
// prevent (docs/MEMORY.md), still being prevented. **Anyone simplifying the trigger down to the
// floor now that the floor is large would reintroduce it invisibly**, which is what this file is
// here to refuse.
//
// Run it directly: `./ziman tests/bench/gcheap/live_growth.zm`.
use clock
use math
use os

struct Row {
    id: int
    name: string
    score: float
}

// A `StructObj` per row in the arena, its `fields` array on Boehm's heap -- the cross-heap shape
// `structs_live` was written for, here with the set growing rather than fixed.
fn build(n: int) -> int {
    val keep = mutable_array_new<Row>(capacity: n)
    val started = clock.monotonic_millis()
    for i in 0..n {
        push(keep, Row { id: i, name: concat("n", str(i)), score: 1.5 })
    }
    val ms = clock.monotonic_millis() - started
    printf("  {} live rows: {} ms, {} KB resident\n", n, ms, switch os.process_memory() {
        Ok(b) { str(b / 1024) }
        Err(_) { "?" }
    })
    return ms
}

fn main() {
    val times = mutable_array_new<int>()
    for n in [200000, 400000, 800000] {
        push(times, build(n))
    }
    val t = snapshot(times)
    val growth = math.max(t[2], 1) / math.max(t[0], 1)
    printf("4x the rows cost {}x the time\n", growth)
    // Linear is 4. Floor-only measured 5.0 and climbing; 8 leaves room for a slow machine without
    // leaving room for the cost class this exists to catch.
    if growth > 8 {
        print("FAIL: a growing live set is no longer linear -- check the collection trigger")
        os.exit(1)
    }
    print("ok   a growing live set stays linear")
}
